---
/** Introduction: what Satin is, who it is for, how it is built and how to use it. */
import Docs from '../layouts/Docs.astro'
import PageHeader from '../components/PageHeader.astro'
import Chip from '../components/Chip.astro'
import { Alert } from '@uisatin/ui/alert'
import { page } from '../nav'

const h2 = 'text-lg font-medium text-text-default'
const prose = 'text-base leading-6 text-text-muted'
const link = 'rounded-sm text-text-default underline underline-offset-2 focus-visible:shadow-border-focus focus-visible:outline-none'
const layers = [
  { title: 'Tokens', text: 'Colour, type, radius, spacing and motion as variables, in Light and Dark. Primitives hold the raw values, semantic tokens give them a job, and nothing else reads a raw value.', href: '/theming' },
  { title: 'Components', text: 'Buttons, fields, menus, dialogs, tables and charts, built on Radix and shadcn/ui and painted only with semantic tokens, so a theme reaches all of them.', href: '/components/button' },
  { title: 'Patterns', text: 'What AI products keep needing: a prompt bar, agent picker, loading and thinking, messages and decisions, composed from the same components.', href: '/ai-patterns/prompt-bar' },
]
---

<Docs current="introduction">
  <PageHeader id="introduction" title={page('introduction').title} description={page('introduction').description} />

  <section aria-labelledby="what-title" class="flex flex-col gap-3 pt-4">
    <h2 id="what-title" class={h2}>What is Satin?</h2>
    <div class="flex flex-col gap-3 text-base leading-6 text-text-muted">
      <p>Satin is a Figma library and React components that match it one to one: same names, same variants, same tokens.</p>
      <Alert
        intent="info"
        title="The Figma library isn't shared yet"
        description="For now Satin is public as React components only. The Figma library will follow later."
      />
      <p>
        The components are built on <a class="text-text-default underline underline-offset-2" href="https://ui.shadcn.com">shadcn/ui</a> and Radix, so roles, keyboard behaviour and focus management come from proven primitives. Satin owns how they look, and with it the visual side of accessibility: a focus ring and text that hold their contrast, target sizes, motion. What it supports and what it doesn't yet is on the <a class="text-text-default underline underline-offset-2" href="/accessibility">Accessibility</a> page.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="who-title" class="flex flex-col gap-3 pt-8">
    <h2 id="who-title" class={h2}>Who is Satin for?</h2>
    <ul class="flex flex-col gap-2 text-base leading-6 text-text-muted">
      <li><span class="font-medium text-text-default">Designers</span> who want to see how a component looks and behaves, in Light and Dark, before they design with it.</li>
      <li><span class="font-medium text-text-default">Engineers</span> who need production code that matches the design without re-creating it by hand.</li>
      <li><span class="font-medium text-text-default">Teams building AI products</span> who need ready answers for prompts, agent progress and decisions.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="built-title" class="flex flex-col gap-3 pt-8">
    <h2 id="built-title" class={h2}>How Satin is built</h2>
    <p class={prose}>Three layers, each built only from the one under it.</p>
    <ol class="grid gap-3 sm:grid-cols-3">
      {
        layers.map((l, i) => (
          <li>
            <a
              href={l.href}
              class="flex h-full flex-col gap-2 rounded-xl border border-border-subtle p-4 transition-colors duration-(--duration-fast) ease-(--ease-out) hover:bg-bg-hover focus-visible:shadow-border-focus focus-visible:outline-none"
            >
              <span class="text-xs text-text-subtle tabular-nums">0{i + 1}</span>
              <span class="text-base font-medium text-text-default">{l.title}</span>
              <span class="text-base leading-6 text-text-muted">{l.text}</span>
            </a>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="how-title" class="flex flex-col gap-3 pt-8">
    <h2 id="how-title" class={h2}>How can I use Satin?</h2>
    <p class={prose}>
      Two ways, and neither needs Tailwind: Satin ships its tokens and every class the components use as one precompiled stylesheet. Copy the components you need into your app with the shadcn CLI and own the code, or install the package and import them. Light and dark mode come from the <Chip>dark</Chip> class on any parent element.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="agent-title" class="flex flex-col gap-3 pt-8">
    <h2 id="agent-title" class={h2}>Install it with your agent</h2>
    <p class={prose}>
      Satin's components are a shadcn registry, so a coding agent can set it up for you. The{' '}
      <a class={link} href="/installation">Installation</a> page has a prompt to paste into Claude Code, Cursor or any agent
      in your repository, and the same steps by hand.
    </p>
  </section>

</Docs>
